use /lib
use /ui
from /scope/planning import Planning
from /ui/components/right-sidebar import TasksTab
from ../editor import Editor
from ../no-project import NoProjectState
from ../open-file import OpenFile
from ../prompt-mode import PromptEditor
from ../prompt-mode/ask-conversation import AskConversation
from ../prompt-mode/chat-input import ChatInput
from ../prompt-mode/message-list import MessageList
from /ui/behaviours/drag-reorder import DragReorderBehaviour
from /ui/components/file-tree import FileTree
from /ui/components/inset-panel import InsetPanel
from /ui/components/left-sidebar import LeftSidebar
from /ui/components/ribbon import Ribbon
from /ui/components/ribbon/planning-tab import RibbonPlanningTab
from /ui/components/right-sidebar import RightSidebar

export ui-component Body:
    pitch:
        The main window's body: the area to the right of the project sidebar,
        where the tasks sent to the harness, and the files opened in tabs
        beside them, are shown, and the chat input they are sent from.

    description:
        Beneath the @{Ribbon}, to the right of the
        @{LeftSidebar}, which runs the full height beside it, and
        reaching the window's right edge. It is part of the @{PromptEditor}.

        The @{ChatInput} lives within the body, along its bottom: its
        left edge against the project sidebar, and above it, beneath the
        body's tab bar, the selected tab's contents: the message list, or an
        open file. While a task runs, the @{RightSidebar} runs
        the body's full height at its right, beside the tab bar, the selected
        tab's contents, and the chat input, and the chat input's right edge is against it: the chat input
        never runs beneath the right sidebar. While that sidebar isn't there,
        the chat input reaches the window's right edge.

        While Planning's tab is selected, neither sidebar shows, and the body
        runs the whole width of the window beneath the ribbon, from the
        window's left edge to its right: the @{LeftSidebar} is hidden whole,
        its view bar and all, and the @{RightSidebar} isn't there either, even
        while a task runs, the queue holds a prompt, or it was opened by hand.
        This is the exception to the left sidebar always being there and to
        when the right sidebar shows. Both go at once, with nothing sliding,
        as the chat input does, as the tabs say, and come back at once when
        Chat, a file, or a task tab is selected, as they were left: the left
        sidebar's view, whether it is collapsed, its width, its scroll, and
        its selection, and the right sidebar's width and whether it was
        opened by hand. If a task started or ended while Planning was
        showing, the right sidebar comes back in whatever state that leaves
        it: there if a task still runs, a prompt is queued, or it was opened
        by hand, and not there otherwise.

        The body is flat and quiet: the selected tab's contents, the message
        list or an open file, sit on the theme's base surface, #444444 in dark
        mode, running edge to edge with no border, and the chat input along
        the bottom is set apart from them only by its own darker tab strip, as
        the @{ChatInput} says, with no line between them.

    sliding:
        Whenever something slides out or back along one of the body's edges,
        as the @{RightSidebar} does, everything else in the body moves
        smoothly with it, frame by frame, rather than jumping once the slide
        is over: the tab bar, the selected tab's contents, the Ask
        conversation while it shows, and the chat input all keep their right edge against the sliding edge throughout, so
        they narrow or widen together, in step with it and with each other.
        None of them lags behind another, is left at its old width until the
        slide settles, or runs beneath what is sliding. Their contents are
        laid out anew at each width as it changes, text wrapping and rows
        reflowing as they go: the tab bar's tabs, the chat input's text and
        buttons, the message list's table, and an open file's editor. A slide
        that reverses partway moves them back from wherever it had reached.

    contents:
        Everything that can be in the body:

        - a tab bar along its top, holding a Chat tab, a tab for each file open, and the Planning tab, right after Chat, once it has been opened, since nothing closes it
        - the @{Planning}, beneath the tab bar while its tab is selected
        - the @{MessageList}, the chat history, shown beneath the tab bar while the Chat tab is selected
        - the hint shown in the message list's place before any task has been sent, or while no project is open, as the @{PromptEditor} and the @{NoProjectState} say
        - an @{Editor} for each open file, shown beneath the tab bar while its tab is selected
        - a task view for each task opened in a tab of its own, as its task tabs say, shown beneath the tab bar while its tab is selected
        - the @{AskConversation}, in the Chat tab's place beneath the tab bar, while the chat input's Ask tab is selected
        - the @{ChatInput}, along the bottom beneath whichever tab is selected but Planning, which is the one tab without it, and beneath the Ask conversation too
        - the @{RightSidebar}, at the right of all of these, the tab bar and the chat input included, while a task runs or is queued, or it is opened by hand

        An @{InsetPanel} floats over the whole window, the body included,
        but is never part of it.

    askMode:
        While the chat input's Ask tab is selected, the Ask conversation, as
        the @{AskConversation} says, takes the Chat tab's place in the
        body, filling all of it beneath the tab bar and above the chat input,
        as the message list fills it for every other mode: the body never
        splits, and nothing is shown beside the conversation. The Chat tab
        then reads "Ask" in Ask's colour. Selecting another mode's tab brings
        the message list back in its place, as it was left, and selecting
        Ask again brings the conversation back, scrolled where it was; the
        switch is at once, with nothing sliding. A file or task tab selected
        shows that file or task whatever mode is selected, and selecting
        Ask while one is shown selects the Chat tab, so the conversation is
        in view.

    tabs:
        Along the top of the body runs a tab bar, in gpui-kit's default tab
        styling, across the width the message list takes: from the project
        sidebar to the right sidebar while a task runs, and to the
        window's right edge otherwise, and from the window's left edge to its
        right while Planning's tab is selected. Its first tab, Chat, shows the message
        list, and is always there: it can't be closed or moved. Once Planning
        has been opened, its tab, "Planning" with a checklist icon, comes
        right after Chat, and is pinned there as Chat is: it has no close
        button, nothing else closes it either, and it can't be dragged, so
        once there it stays in the bar. After them comes a tab
        for each open file and each task opened in a tab of its own, files
        and tasks together, in the order they were opened unless they have
        been dragged since, scrolling sideways once there are more than fit.

        Planning's tab is opened from the ribbon: opening the ribbon's
        Planning tab, by clicking it, with Alt+2, or by Ctrl+clicking it to
        open it alongside other ribbon tabs, opens Planning's tab here and
        selects it, or selects it if it is already open, as the
        @{RibbonPlanningTab} says. Opening another ribbon tab, or closing the
        ribbon's Planning tab with Ctrl+click, changes nothing here: Planning's
        tab stays in the bar and the selected tab stays selected.

        Just one tab's contents show at a time, filling the space between the
        tab bar and the chat input. The chat input stays along the bottom
        whichever tab is selected but Planning, so a prompt can be written
        while a file or a task shows. The body starts with Chat selected, and
        clicking a tab selects it.

        Planning is the one tab without the chat input: while its tab is
        selected, the chat input isn't shown, nor is either sidebar, as the
        description says, and Planning fills the body from the tab bar to the
        body's bottom, across the whole width of the window. Selecting Chat, a file, or a
        task tab brings the chat input back straight away, along the bottom
        as before. Nothing slides: it goes and comes back at once, and the
        selected tab's contents take or give up its room at once too. Hiding
        it keeps all it holds: its draft text, its attachments, and its
        selected mode tab are there again, as they were, when it shows. If
        it had keyboard focus when Planning was selected, it has keyboard
        focus again when it shows.

        Every tab in the bar, Chat and each file's alike, is laid out the
        same plain way, with nothing extra around it: the bar's full height,
        32 pixels, its contents centred up and down in it, 12 pixels of
        padding at its left, and 12 at its right, and no margin of its own
        above, below, or beside it, so one tab ends where the next begins,
        with only the tab styling's own line between them. Nothing is inset
        from the bar's top or bottom, and the first tab starts at the bar's
        left edge.

        A file's tab shows the file's name, then, while it has unsaved
        changes, a dot, and then a small close button, an icon button 16
        pixels square, set close beside the name, 4 pixels from it or from
        the dot, with no more room between them however long or short the
        name is. Since the button has room of its own around its icon, a file
        tab's right padding is 6 pixels rather than 12, so the icon sits as
        far from the tab's right edge as the name does from its left; the tab
        is only as wide as its padding, name, dot, and close button need.
        Chat's spinner, while something runs, sits 6 pixels after its label,
        inside the same padding. Hovering a file's tab shows the file's path
        in the project as its tooltip.

        A file's tab can be dragged along the tab bar to reorder the files, as
        the @{DragReorderBehaviour} says, a vertical line showing the gap
        between two tabs it will land in, and the tab dragged following the
        pointer as the file's name. Chat stays first, and Planning, while its
        tab is there, right after it: no gap before a file's or task's first
        tab, between Chat or Planning and it, shows the indicator, so no file
        or task tab is ever dropped in front of either, and neither Chat nor
        Planning can be dragged.
        Dragging a tab selects it, as clicking it does, and the selection
        follows the tab to its new place; nothing else about its editor
        changes. A press on a tab's close button closes it rather than
        starting a drag. A file opened afterwards still gets its new tab just
        after the one selected, or, while Chat is selected and Planning's tab
        is there, just after Planning's, never between the two.

    taskTabs:
        The Chat tab shows only the task the harness is working on now, and
        those running beside it, as the @{MessageList}'s header shows them,
        or, while none runs, the task that finished last, until the next is
        sent. It never shows any other task: a task opened from the right
        sidebar's Tasks tab, as the @{TasksTab} says, or from anywhere
        else that opens a task, opens in a tab of its own in the body's tab
        bar, as a file does: a task not open yet gets a new tab just after
        the one selected, and is selected; opening one that already has a
        tab selects that tab; and opening the task Chat is showing selects
        Chat instead.

        A task's tab is laid out as a file's is: a bar 2 pixels wide down
        its left in its mode's colour, its status, a spinner while it is
        under way, a tick, cross, or dash once it has ended, then its name,
        the first line of its prompt cut off with an ellipsis at 24
        characters, then its close button. Hovering it shows its prompt's
        first lines and its mode as its tooltip. It can be dragged among the
        files' tabs, and closed as they are, closing it changing nothing
        about the task.

        Beneath it, the task shows as it would at the head of the Chat tab,
        on its own: its header, with its status, time, compiled prompt, and
        buttons, then its output table, then the files it changed, scrolled
        to its top the first time it is shown, and as it was left after. A
        task still running shows its output as it comes, its table locked to
        the bottom while scrolled there, as the latest task's is, and
        carries on in its tab whether or not the tab is selected. Its tab
        stays open once it finishes. Opening a task in a tab doesn't select
        it in the Chat tab or change what Chat shows, and Chat's running
        tasks are never opened in tabs of their own unless asked for, a
        running task opened from the Tasks tab selecting it in Chat's
        header instead. Each open project keeps its own task tabs, as its
        file tabs are kept, and none is reopened when the project is
        opened again.

    openFile:
        A file opened, from the @{FileTree} or anywhere else the
        @{OpenFile} says, shows in an @{Editor} in a tab of its own,
        as many files open at once as are opened. A file not open yet gets a
        new tab, just after the one selected, and is selected. Opening a file
        that already has a tab selects that tab instead, leaving its editor
        as it was, unsaved changes and all.

        Nothing slides: a file's tab is selected, and its editor shows, at
        once, and closing it takes it away at once too. Files never split the
        body among themselves; the file takes the tab bar's full width above
        the chat input.

        Closing a file's tab, with its close button or the editor's, closes
        the file, the editor first asking to discard any unsaved changes.
        Closing the selected tab selects the tab to its right, or, with none
        to its right, the one to its left, which is Planning, while its tab
        is there, or else Chat, once no file is left. Closing a tab that isn't selected leaves the selection as it
        is.

